# Theme Versioning and Changelog Rules

These rules control how completed theme changes are versioned and documented.

## Version Source of Truth

The official WordPress theme version lives in:

`style.css`

Inside the root theme header:

`Version:            X.X.X`

Do not treat `package.json`, `composer.json`, lock files, compiled asset filenames, or deployment tags as the official WordPress theme version unless the user explicitly says otherwise.

## Changelog Source of Truth

The changelog lives in:

`CHANGELOG.md`

Every completed version bump must have a matching changelog entry.

The changelog version must match the version in `style.css`.

## Starting Version

New projects start at:

`1.0.0`

## Semantic Versioning

Use semantic versioning.

Patch version:

- Small fixes
- Styling fixes
- Accessibility fixes
- Copy updates
- Template adjustments
- Bug fixes
- Small behavior corrections

Example:

`1.0.0` to `1.0.1`

Minor version:

- Completed new Gutenberg blocks
- Completed new templates
- Completed new reusable components
- Completed new theme sections
- Completed plugin integrations
- Completed feature work

Example:

`1.0.1` to `1.1.0`

Major version:

- Breaking changes
- Large architecture changes
- Major compatibility changes
- Major theme restructuring

Example:

`1.1.0` to `2.0.0`

## Iteration Rule

Do not bump the theme version for every small adjustment during an active task.

If the user is actively building or refining the same Gutenberg block, template, component, layout, or feature, do not bump the version for each iteration.

Only bump the version when the full change set is complete.

A version bump represents a completed change set, not every message, edit, or adjustment.

## When to Bump

Bump the version when:

- A requested feature is complete.
- A requested fix is complete.
- A requested Gutenberg block is complete.
- A requested template is complete.
- A requested integration is complete.
- The user says the current task is done.
- The user moves on to a separate work area after the current work is complete.

Do not bump the version when:

- The user is still iterating on the same task.
- The user asks for spacing, color, field, copy, layout, or visual tweaks within the same active task.
- The user asks for planning only.
- The user asks for a prompt only.
- The user asks for recommendations only.
- The work is exploratory and not accepted yet.

## Required style.css Header

When creating a new `style.css`, use the project values from:

`.cursor/project-settings.md`

The required header format is:

/*
Theme Name:         [Project Name] - Theme
Theme URI:          [Project URL]
Description:        Theme based on Sage 11.0.1
Version:            1.0.0
Author:             Samir Joza
Author URI:         https://samirjoza.dev
Text Domain:        sage
License:            MIT License
License URI:        https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT
Requires PHP:       8.2
Requires at least:  6.6
*/

Replace placeholder values with the project-specific values from `.cursor/project-settings.md`.

## Changelog Format

Use this changelog format:

# Changelog

All notable completed theme changes should be documented in this file.

## [1.0.1] - YYYY-MM-DD

### Added

- Description of newly added functionality.

### Changed

- Description of completed changes.

### Fixed

- Description of completed fixes.

Only include sections that apply.

Allowed sections:

- Added
- Changed
- Fixed
- Removed
- Security

## Version Bump Workflow

When a completed change set requires a version bump:

1. Read `.cursor/project-settings.md`.
2. Read the current version from `style.css`.
3. Decide whether the completed change is patch, minor, or major.
4. Update only the `Version:` field in `style.css`.
5. Add a matching entry to `CHANGELOG.md`.
6. Use the current date for the changelog entry.
7. Keep the changelog entry clear and human-readable.
8. Do not log every iteration from the same active task.
9. Do not create fake history.

## Final Check

Before completing a versioned task:

- Confirm `style.css` has the correct version.
- Confirm `CHANGELOG.md` has the same version.
- Confirm the changelog date is correct.
- Confirm the version was bumped only once for the completed change set.